  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nPattern / Forecast Synopsis: \n\nVertically stacked surface low over James Bay to continue its north \nand east motion, forcing a surface cold front through the region as \nexpansive 1024mb surface high pressure intrudes hot on its heels. \nMeanwhile, expansive thermal ridge over the southeastern CONUS will \ncontinue its dominance in those parts, leading to the aforementioned \ncold front to largely stall out as a stationary front roughly along \nthe I-80 corridor between Omaha and Cleveland. As such, deepest \nmoisture to be suppressed well to our south through Friday as pseudo-\nzonal flow around the ridge leads to some serious rainfall amounts \nto our south. Weak northern stream perturbation over the northern \nRockies zips east along the US-Canada border, arriving quite \nmoisture starved tonight into early Thursday. Surface high pressure \nto return through the day Thursday, but short lived. \n\nWave with tropical Pacific origins to surge into the desert SW today \n(likely bringing a serious flooding threat to Arizona and much of \nthe Four Corners region). It will then eject into the northern \nPlains by Friday, evolving into a convectively charged wave rich \nwith deep, Pacific moisture as it moves through the Midwest toward \nthe Great Lakes region. WNW flow regime over the northern CONUS \nlooks to force this wave south as it progresses toward the Great \nLakes, likely leaving the highest moisture concentrations (and thus, \nhighest probs of heavy rain, and perhaps even rain in general) just \nsouth of northern Michigan... but may nose far enough north to lead \nto some rains across the bottom rows of counties in the CWA. \n\nDetails: \n\nNot a lot to speak of during the day today as drier air settles in \nwith seasonable highs in the upper 60s to mid 70s amid peeks of \nafternoon sun. Clouds increase again tonight as the upper level \nnorthern stream wave makes a pass through the region. Better \nmoisture to our west should allow showers to maintain themselves \ninto the northwest lower shores of Lake Michigan... but as it moves \ninto a more hostile environment lacking moisture and instability, \nanticipation is that shower intensity and coverage decreases \nconsiderably as activity progresses eastward. Rainfall amounts will \nlikely struggle to eclipse 0.10\" anyways, so little to no impacts \nanticipated. Drier weather to prevail Thursday and Friday with \nseasonable temperatures in the 60s-70s, lows in the 40s-50s Thursday \nnight. \n\nAforementioned Pacific wave to make its presence known later Friday \ninto Friday night / Saturday as upstream rain and thunderstorms \napproach. Moisture profiles are strongly signaling heavy rainfall \npotential in any convective elements... but will have to see if \ntheta-e advection is strong enough to draw the aforementioned \nstationary front northward to provide better forcing for that to \nhappen here. Current guidance is laying down an impressive stripe of \nrain (to the order of 2-5\"+ of rain) with this wave from Minnesota / \nIowa, ESE into Wisconsin / Illinois, and lands more downstate in \nMichigan as opposed to up here. Residual moisture from this wave \ncould spark some showers at times, but current thinking is the \nheaviest rainfall across the region would likely hold south of M-72. \nThis is further echoed by latest WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlooks... \nduring the Tuesday afternoon forecast package, all of northern lower \nwas in a Slight Risk (2/4) and eastern upper in Marginal (1/4). \nLatest outlook suppresses the Slight Risk south of I-96, and the \nMarginal Risk cuts off roughly along M-32... signaling high \nconfidence in this southern turn. Regardless, rain chances to creep \nback into the picture Friday night and again Saturday... but \ntentative thinking is that perhaps we sneak out a drier weekend as \nopposed to a soggy one, especially the farther north one goes. More \ndetails to come.",
